<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Benin</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Republic of Benin, Dahomey</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benin-empire">Benin</a> is a country in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-africa">West Africa</a>, bordered by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/togo">Togo</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nigeria">Nigeria</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burkina-faso">Burkina Faso</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/niger-river">Niger</a>, with its coast on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bight-of-benin">Bight of Benin</a>. From the 17th to the 19th centuries the area held political entities including the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-dahomey">Kingdom of Dahomey</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/city-state">city-state</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/porto">Porto</a> Novo. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> took over the territory in 1894 as French Dahomey, and it became independent on 1 August 1960. A Marxist–Leninist coup led to its renaming as Benin in 1975, after the Bight of Benin. The People's Republic of Benin lasted from 1975 to 1990 and was replaced in 1991 by a multi-party republic.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benin-empire">Benin</a> is a north–south strip of land in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-africa">West Africa</a> between latitudes 6° and 13°N. It is about 650 kilometres from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/niger-river">Niger River</a> in the north to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlantic-ocean">Atlantic Ocean</a> in the south, but its coastline measures only 121 kilometres and the country is about 325 kilometres wide at its widest point. Most of the population lives on the southern coast of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bight-of-benin">Bight of Benin</a>, part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gulf-of-guinea">Gulf of Guinea</a>. Four ecoregions lie within its borders: Eastern Guinean forests, Nigerian lowland forests, the Guinean forest-savanna mosaic and West Sudanian savanna. The country is tropical, and its economy depends on agriculture, above all <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotton">cotton</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Before 1600 the area held <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/city-state">city-states</a> along the coast, mainly of the Aja, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yoruba">Yoruba</a> and Gbe peoples, and inland regions of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bariba">Bariba</a>, Mahi, Gedevi and Kabye peoples. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oyo-empire">Oyo Empire</a>, to the east, raided and exacted tribute from the coastal kingdoms. In the 17th and 18th centuries the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-dahomey">Kingdom of Dahomey</a>, made up mostly of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fon-people">Fon people</a>, arose on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abomey">Abomey</a> plateau and began taking over the coast. By 1727 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/agaja">King Agaja</a> had conquered the coastal cities of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/allada">Allada</a> and Whydah. Dahomey had become a tributary of Oyo and rivalled, but did not directly attack, the Oyo-allied city-state of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/porto-novo">Porto-Novo</a>.</p><p>Under French rule, French Dahomey was included in French West Africa in 1899. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> appears to have found the colony lacking the agricultural or mineral resources for large-scale capitalist development and treated it as a preserve in case later discoveries justified investment. After independence on 1 August 1960 the country was named Dahomey, and coups and changes of regime followed. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hubert-maga">Hubert Maga</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sourou-migan-apithy">Sourou Migan Apithy</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/justin-ahomadegbe-tometin">Justin Ahomadégbé</a> and Émile Derlin Zinsou dominated politics; the first three each represented a different region and ethnic group, and they formed a Presidential Council after violence marred the 1970 elections. On 30 November 1975, after a Marxist–Leninist military coup, the country was renamed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benin-empire">Benin</a>, since the name Dahomey is associated only with the Fon.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The capital is <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/porto-novo">Porto-Novo</a>, while the seat of government is in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotonou">Cotonou</a>, the most populous city and economic capital. The official language is French, and Fon, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bariba">Bariba</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yoruba">Yoruba</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dendi">Dendi</a> are among the indigenous languages spoken. The country is divided into twelve <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">departments</a> and 77 communes. About 42 ethnic groups live there, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> are the two main religions, with Vodun and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orisha">Orisha</a> beliefs also held. Many non-sovereign monarchies survive from pre-colonial kingdoms, and a law of 2025 recognised 16 sub-national kingdoms.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Benin” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
